    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, trying to frame it as a mitigation issue rather than a violation. My concern is the \"encroachment\" language itself in the ordinance. It sounds almost aggressive, like the city treats any minor boundary overlap as a hostile intrusion or something that needs to be purged. Is there a way to address the concept of boundaries and these types of intrusions without making it sound like the land is being compromised or invaded by these external elements?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Instead of using the term \"encroachment,\" reframe the issue using neutral planning language like \"boundary delineation variance\" or \"site interface condition.\" Emphasize that these are pre-existing, long-standing conditions on the property line that existing neighbors have tolerated. Your argument should pivot to how the proposed mixed-use design thoughtfully incorporates or accepts this existing interface feature rather than fighting against it.",
      "source": "target",
